1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Run faster, jump higher, play longer. It's the ultimate goal of every athlete.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
But when training and preparation run their course, science steps up to the plate.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:23,000
It's a world where success comes not from the sweat of the player, but the brains of an engineer.

4
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, Sports Technology on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:51,491 --> 00:00:57,020
In the fast-paced world of sports, the use of technology to enhance performance and improve

6
00:00:57,021 --> 00:00:58,940
safety has become common.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Lighter materials have decreased the bulk and weight of equipment.

8
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:11,000
Computer-aided design has pushed the limits of what was once thought impossible.

9
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:15,000
For a particular athlete, we can look and say, you know, here's the shape of your arch,

10
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:21,000
and we can make a shoe that fits precisely the way your foot is shaped and also how your foot moves.

11
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:29,000
Both professional and amateur athletes have benefited from engineers coming up with new ways to improve sports.

12
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:38,000
Runner's speeds increase, batted balls go farther, and the risk for many injuries has gone down dramatically.

13
00:01:38,001 --> 00:01:44,000
And a new kind of excitement enters a game when technology takes a swing.

14
00:01:44,001 --> 00:01:52,000
The same science that can catch speeders on a freeway can clock the velocity of a 100-mile-per-hour pitch.

15
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:57,000
Radar guns have been used in the major leagues since the late 1980s,

16
00:01:57,001 --> 00:02:01,000
but now the radar baseball has arrived.

17
00:02:01,001 --> 00:02:06,000
What we've done is taking a traditional baseball and put computer technology in it

18
00:02:06,001 --> 00:02:09,000
that will actually tell you how fast you throw this when you throw it.

19
00:02:09,001 --> 00:02:14,000
What's inside there is a switch that when the pitcher actually releases the ball,

20
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:18,000
it turns on that sensor. And then when it impacts the glove or the catcher,

21
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:20,200
or the catcher, it'll shut down that sensor.

22
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:23,960
Then it will transfer the time it went from on to off,

23
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:26,720
from the pitcher's mound to home plate, into miles per hour,

24
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:29,320
right here on an LCD display, right on the baseball.

25
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,600
Though not recommended for batting practice,

26
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:35,960
the baseballs are a big help, especially to little leaguers.

27
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:41,080
Pitchers can monitor their success without needing an expensive radar gun.

28
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,820
In other sports, great changes have taken place to allow athletes

29
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:49,520
to cover longer distances than ever before.

30
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:52,600
This is especially true in cycling.

31
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:56,500
The lightness makes it so that you don't work as hard

32
00:02:56,501 --> 00:02:57,520
and can travel further.

33
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:01,920
And with gears and the newer shock-absorbing systems,

34
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:05,400
you can literally travel great distances on bicycles,

35
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:07,260
something that you would never have done

36
00:03:07,261 --> 00:03:11,480
with a coaster bicycle that I grew up on when I was a kid.

37
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:15,040
Some sports even owe their very existence

38
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:16,680
to improve technology.

39
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:23,820
In the 1980s, an inventor from Minnesota wanted to find a way to train for hockey year-round.

40
00:03:23,821 --> 00:03:32,820
He had the bright idea that if you moved the wheels on a pair of skates and put them in a straight line rather than two by two,

41
00:03:32,821 --> 00:03:37,820
you had something that at least resembled a little bit, an ice hockey skate.

42
00:03:37,821 --> 00:03:43,820
With some marketing skills, it moved on to become something that was seen as very good fitness,

43
00:03:43,821 --> 00:03:50,820
seen as a substitute for bicycle riding, became part of what we call just recreational inline skating.

44
00:03:50,821 --> 00:03:57,820
In practically every area of sports, technology has helped revolutionize how the games are played.

45
00:03:57,821 --> 00:04:02,820
Most of those changes have come within the last 50 years,

46
00:04:02,821 --> 00:04:08,820
but the use of technology to enhance performance is at least 2,000 years old.

47
00:04:08,821 --> 00:04:16,820
In the first Olympic games, wrestlers protected their hands from being cut in the heat of battle by wrapping their arms in cloth.

48
00:04:16,821 --> 00:04:23,820
Modern hockey masks and face cages for football trace their roots to the Knights of the Middle Ages.

49
00:04:23,821 --> 00:04:34,820
Face plates developed to protect Knights in battle were also used during sports such as jousting to protect the athlete and intimidate an opponent.

50
00:04:34,821 --> 00:04:41,820
The Native Americans of what is now the New England states used tools from the forest to play their games.

51
00:04:41,821 --> 00:04:47,820
They used pieces of wood to hit a wooden ball similar to modern day field hockey.

52
00:04:47,821 --> 00:04:55,820
In 1891 in Springfield, Massachusetts, the first basket used in the first basketball game was just that,

53
00:04:55,821 --> 00:05:00,820
a peach basket into which players tossed a soccer ball.

54
00:05:00,821 --> 00:05:07,820
early baseball players learned the hard way about what a fast-moving ball could do to the palms of their hands.

55
00:05:07,821 --> 00:05:11,820
Simple gloves made of leather quickly became the norm.

56
00:05:11,821 --> 00:05:17,820
The ancient sport of golf was born on the moors of Scotland 500 years ago.

57
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:21,820
Golfers came up with ingenious ways to play their game.

58
00:05:21,821 --> 00:05:23,820
They used bent sticks for clubs.

59
00:05:23,821 --> 00:05:29,820
Early golf balls were nothing more than tufts of bird feathers held together with twine.

60
00:05:29,821 --> 00:05:37,363
twine. Today, everything from drivers to putters are made of high-tech metals.

61
00:05:37,364 --> 00:05:39,200
Shafts have evolved

62
00:05:39,201 --> 00:05:45,800
from steel to aluminum to titanium, all in an effort to go farther and be more accurate.

63
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:52,720
For a pro, it may make his game better, but for the consumer, it makes the game more enjoyable.

64
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,700
Just the ability to keep the ball in the fairway a few extra times is a great pleasure for the

65
00:05:59,701 --> 00:06:05,626
average golfer. Today's better sports technology comes at a price.

66
00:06:05,627 --> 00:06:07,960
It costs more to equip the

67
00:06:07,961 --> 00:06:13,120
average athlete, and in spite of the increased protection the technology provides, injuries,

68
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:16,698
even for amateur athletes, can be much more severe.

69
00:06:16,699 --> 00:06:19,300
We've all seen those horrendous pictures

70
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:25,240
where the leg is going one way and the athletes going the other, and that tends to be because

71
00:06:25,241 --> 00:06:31,000
we're pushing our bodies more, and technology's allowed us to push it a little bit farther.

72
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:35,760
So you're not seeing isolated ligament injuries, you're seeing multiple ligament injuries,

73
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:39,240
you're seeing complex bony fractures where it's sticking through the skin,

74
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:43,014
instead of the simple fractures that keep us out.

75
00:06:43,015 --> 00:06:46,000
So as we're progressing, the technology's there

76
00:06:46,001 --> 00:06:50,171
to protect us, but when something happens, it's usually pretty severe.

77
00:06:50,172 --> 00:06:51,660
In the first half of this

78
00:06:51,661 --> 00:06:57,360
century there were fewer chances for players to sustain serious injury simply because the

79
00:06:57,361 --> 00:07:03,740
technology of sports had not advanced far enough to create high speeds and bone-crushing impacts.

80
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:11,140
It would all change when soldiers fighting in World War II would find a way to incorporate

81
00:07:11,141 --> 00:07:14,540
their military training into the world of sports.

82
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:26,580
Aluminum baseball bats were developed during the 1960s as a way for Little League teams to save the cost of shattered wooden bats.

83
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:36,240
Skiing, the ability to soar down snow-covered mountains at a high rate of speed.

84
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:42,740
Technological challenge, upgrade speed, maneuverability, and safety all at the same time.

85
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:51,540
Locked deep within the peat bogs of Sweden and Finland, under 5,000 years of ice and snow,

86
00:07:51,541 --> 00:07:57,540
lay the remnants of one of the oldest sports known to man, skiing.

87
00:07:57,541 --> 00:08:03,540
The ancient Nordics used slabs of wood to traverse long distances in search of food

88
00:08:03,541 --> 00:08:06,540
and for the enjoyment of traveling over snow.

89
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:11,540
It was a primitive technology that would last thousands of years.

90
00:08:11,541 --> 00:08:15,260
But skiing as a sport has more modern origins.

91
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:21,940
The first recorded competition was a cross-country ski race held in Norway in 1843.

92
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:28,980
A half century later, the first international downhill skiing exhibition was held there in 1892.

93
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:32,300
By then, skis were being shaped differently.

94
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:36,720
The front tips arched upward to give an athlete more speed on the slopes.

95
00:08:36,721 --> 00:08:43,600
about the same time in the united states skiing took root in communities where scandinavians

96
00:08:43,601 --> 00:08:50,000
settled especially in wisconsin and minnesota way back when people were skiing on relatively

97
00:08:50,001 --> 00:08:55,600
primitive wooden planks lacing the planks onto their feet and wearing leather boots that were

98
00:08:55,601 --> 00:09:02,720
also lace-up boots in fact my first ski boots were lace-up boots i'm embarrassed to say and from there

99
00:09:02,721 --> 00:09:07,760
the boots evolved, the bindings evolved, and certainly the skis evolved as well.

100
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:12,560
But the old-fashioned wooden ski often caused irreparable damage

101
00:09:12,561 --> 00:09:15,720
to those unlucky enough to take a tumble on the slopes.

102
00:09:16,780 --> 00:09:21,040
When we first realized that we can go downhill in snow,

103
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:26,460
we put these wooden planks on our feet, and they were very loosely attached to our shoe.

104
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:29,660
As time progressed, they became more rigidly attached,

105
00:09:29,661 --> 00:09:35,480
and then we started seeing more injuries because we now have a long lever arm attached to our foot,

106
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:39,280
which puts all kinds of forces on the knee joint and the hip joint.

107
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:45,460
In fact, new technology would affect areas related to skiing,

108
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:49,480
such as the development of ski lifts and tow ropes to get up mountains,

109
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:53,240
long before any attempt was made to improve the ski.

110
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:58,340
Everything changed during the alpine battles of World War II.

111
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:04,160
American soldiers were trained to ski on old wooden slats

112
00:10:04,161 --> 00:10:07,140
to ensure they could fight in European mountains.

113
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:11,640
There was relatively little skiing of any sort in the United States

114
00:10:11,641 --> 00:10:13,140
prior to the Second World War,

115
00:10:13,141 --> 00:10:21,080
but because in Europe we thought we might be fighting in a mountain environment,

116
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:25,200
we trained American soldiers on skis.

117
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:29,120
They learned on wood slats with leather boots.

118
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:34,380
When the war ended, the soldiers headed home, and they took their skis with them.

119
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:42,780
These people, as they came out of the Second World War, they found something that they liked to do,

120
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:45,940
but they weren't happy with the equipment on which they were doing it.

121
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:52,500
And so you begin to have, first of all, someone putting metal edges on the wooden boards that they went down the slope,

122
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:54,260
which really improved the product.

123
00:10:54,261 --> 00:11:01,400
In the post-war United States, going down a snow-covered hill at 40 miles an hour caught on fast.

124
00:11:02,920 --> 00:11:06,320
As more people tried skiing, the equipment improved.

125
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:10,540
Most of the changes in technology came from the skiers themselves.

126
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:17,740
There were avid skiers out there who had the minds of engineers and noodlers and inventors

127
00:11:17,741 --> 00:11:23,860
who decided that they no longer wanted to leash their boots to their skis and bindings

128
00:11:23,861 --> 00:11:26,020
and wanted to have releasable bindings,

129
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:30,500
or who decided it was much easier to buckle a boot than to tie it,

130
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:34,820
and who felt that the shape and the length of their skis deserved a little work

131
00:11:34,821 --> 00:11:39,420
and that the end result would be faster performance or better grip on snow.

132
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:44,320
But as ski technology improved, skiers began to suffer.

133
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:47,480
The increased speed created by better skis,

134
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,220
either by improved design or materials,

135
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:52,780
created havoc for amateur skiers.

136
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:58,860
By the 1960s, skiers who had trained on wooden skis at relatively low speeds

137
00:11:58,861 --> 00:12:05,860
could soar down mountainsides on plastic-coated skis traveling more than 60 miles per hour.

138
00:12:05,861 --> 00:12:10,860
Skiing technology would have to find a way to prevent injuries.

139
00:12:10,861 --> 00:12:18,860
Skiing injuries to the thumb is a common thing that we used to see because the old ski poles had straps.

140
00:12:18,861 --> 00:12:24,580
and they found out that the straps that we had around our wrists were putting a lever effect on the thumb,

141
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:26,520
tearing the ligaments when we fall.

142
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:32,940
So they changed the equipment, and we see less and less of those types of injuries.

143
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:40,260
Throughout the 1970s and 80s, new materials continued to improve a skier's ability

144
00:12:40,261 --> 00:12:44,080
to safely move downhill sides at top speeds.

145
00:12:44,081 --> 00:12:51,080
Today it is not uncommon for skiers to go 70 or 80 miles an hour, gliding on fiberglass

146
00:12:51,081 --> 00:12:55,080
skis that have almost zero friction against the snow.

147
00:12:55,081 --> 00:13:00,080
But perhaps the greatest advancements in terms of safety have come in the bindings that keep

148
00:13:00,081 --> 00:13:06,080
an athlete's foot attached to a ski.

149
00:13:06,081 --> 00:13:09,080
In the past, skiers were literally lashed to a ski.

150
00:13:09,081 --> 00:13:11,080
If they fell, the ski would remain

151
00:13:11,081 --> 00:13:14,829
remain bound to their foot causing legs to snap.

152
00:13:14,830 --> 00:13:17,640
Breakaway bindings allowed skiers to

153
00:13:17,641 --> 00:13:20,756
release from their skis when tumbling down a hill.

154
00:13:20,757 --> 00:13:23,000
The bindings were clamps which would

155
00:13:23,001 --> 00:13:28,280
release the foot from the ski if an extreme amount of sideways pressure was placed on the leg of the

156
00:13:28,281 --> 00:13:28,981
skier.

157
00:13:28,982 --> 00:13:34,600
But it wasn't until the 1990s that the concept of breakaway bindings evolved to the

158
00:13:34,601 --> 00:13:36,552
point of near perfection.

159
00:13:36,553 --> 00:13:41,160
As the bindings have progressed through time, they've given

160
00:13:41,161 --> 00:13:45,678
free freedom from your foot and the board or the ski.

161
00:13:45,679 --> 00:13:48,320
And they originally were in one

162
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:52,131
dimension lifting up with your heel. Now, then they came two-dimensional.

163
00:13:52,132 --> 00:13:53,280
Now, three-dimensional

164
00:13:53,281 --> 00:14:00,280
bindings have progressed and technology's been answered for the out-of-control skier

165
00:14:00,281 --> 00:14:04,280
skier who's falling to prevent injuries.

166
00:14:04,281 --> 00:14:09,280
Now a new technology has begun to revolutionize the skiing industry.

167
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:18,280
It's called snowboarding, the use of one fiberglass ski to glide down mountains.

168
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:26,280
It was born from an idea to combine skateboarding with the speed of downhill skiing.

169
00:14:26,281 --> 00:14:32,960
Today, it is a multi-million dollar sport with hundreds of thousands of amateur enthusiasts

170
00:14:32,961 --> 00:14:39,340
and professional tournaments.

171
00:14:39,341 --> 00:14:44,560
But there have been other changes in skiing that are even more revolutionary.

172
00:14:44,561 --> 00:14:49,800
The aerospace industry has contributed design techniques that help make better equipment.

173
00:14:49,801 --> 00:14:55,100
It is amazing how advanced the technologies are in sports today.

174
00:14:55,101 --> 00:14:58,840
I remember once when we developed a ski, probably in about 1993,

175
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:03,480
we actually did vibration testing on it in the Boeing Space Lab.

176
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,960
It was the first time they'd ever tested sports equipment in there,

177
00:15:07,060 --> 00:15:09,460
but we wanted to see if it could handle the same vibrations

178
00:15:09,461 --> 00:15:12,440
that were being felt by spacecraft when they re-enter the atmosphere.

179
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:18,160
Skis now have shock absorbers, making the ride smoother and faster.

180
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:21,660
Developed by ski manufacturer K2,

181
00:15:21,661 --> 00:15:28,420
piezo technology named after its inventor helps dissipate vibration as the ski moves downhill.

182
00:15:29,420 --> 00:15:34,988
The piezo is actually a ceramic unit and it's quite small.

183
00:15:34,989 --> 00:15:38,060
It looks like a wafer and it has

184
00:15:38,061 --> 00:15:45,100
been used in jet fighters and all kinds of other equipment where extreme sensitivity to vibration

185
00:15:45,101 --> 00:15:47,040
needs to take place.

186
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:50,380
And this tiny sensor inside this ceramic

187
00:15:50,381 --> 00:15:56,380
is able to basically take energy of one kind

188
00:15:56,381 --> 00:15:59,900
and turn it into another energy by dissipating it.

189
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:03,760
And instead of skidding like crazy over the ice

190
00:16:03,761 --> 00:16:06,160
or maybe losing control a little bit,

191
00:16:06,760 --> 00:16:10,380
the piezo inside the ski absorbs the energy

192
00:16:10,381 --> 00:16:12,480
that that roughness causes,

193
00:16:12,481 --> 00:16:17,160
the result being that you were able to function better on the ski

194
00:16:17,161 --> 00:16:19,600
almost as if you had a shock absorber in it.

195
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:22,600
When vibration travels through a ski,

196
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,100
it is captured by the porous piezo ceramic chip,

197
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:29,580
which acts like a sponge absorbing the energy

198
00:16:29,581 --> 00:16:31,500
before it is felt by the skier.

199
00:16:36,260 --> 00:16:40,100
While important in skiing, the concept of absorbing shock

200
00:16:40,101 --> 00:16:45,180
would find its most crucial test far from the snowy hillside domain of skiers.

201
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:51,620
Next, the realm of the runner would be one of sports technology's greatest tests.

202
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:58,840
The first ski-making factory was built in 1854 in California

203
00:16:58,841 --> 00:17:03,860
by gold seekers who skied to break the monotony of mining for gold.

204
00:17:03,861 --> 00:17:14,160
Running, short sprints or long jogs for fitness and pleasure, technological challenge, create

205
00:17:14,161 --> 00:17:17,580
comfort and endurance while protecting athletes from injury.

206
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:25,000
One industry that has greatly benefited from the use of technology and its equipment is

207
00:17:25,001 --> 00:17:26,000
the shoe business.

208
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,160
Today, millions of people jog and run each year for pleasure and for fitness.

209
00:17:32,161 --> 00:17:39,160
The demand for better equipment has been driven by a desire for comfort and safety.

210
00:17:39,161 --> 00:17:42,160
Runners can suffer a multitude of injuries.

211
00:17:42,161 --> 00:17:48,160
Shin splints and tendinitis sideline hundreds of thousands of runners each year.

212
00:17:48,161 --> 00:17:55,160
And more severe injuries such as damage to the knee and back can put runners out of commission for extended periods of time.

213
00:17:55,161 --> 00:18:02,160
The challenge for shoe manufacturers is to provide maximum protection along with the most comfortable shoe possible.

214
00:18:02,161 --> 00:18:04,388
Well, shoe technology, it's very interesting.

215
00:18:04,389 --> 00:18:07,160
It's evolved, but in a lot of ways it's stayed the same.

216
00:18:07,161 --> 00:18:12,160
Key components to the shoes have gotten lighter, more cushioning benefits, longer lasting.

217
00:18:12,161 --> 00:18:20,160
But really still the key to the product is the age-old developing the right last that the shoe forms around

218
00:18:20,161 --> 00:18:24,160
and the little techniques that are used to make the shoe really comfortable.

219
00:18:24,161 --> 00:18:30,092
comfortable. Running shoes and other variations on the sneaker are relatively new.

220
00:18:30,093 --> 00:18:30,960
They've been

221
00:18:30,961 --> 00:18:33,328
around for the past 30 years.

222
00:18:33,329 --> 00:18:38,880
But in its infancy, the sport of running offered no protection to an

223
00:18:38,881 --> 00:18:44,168
athlete. During the ancient Olympics, runners ran marathons in their bare feet.

224
00:18:44,169 --> 00:18:45,440
For the better part

225
00:18:45,441 --> 00:18:49,454
of history, shoes were worn only by the wealthy or by soldiers.

226
00:18:49,455 --> 00:18:51,620
But there were attempts to protect

227
00:18:51,621 --> 00:18:53,270
the feet of an athlete.

228
00:18:53,271 --> 00:18:58,220
In the ancient world, thong sandals with flat soles of papyrus strips

229
00:18:58,221 --> 00:18:59,605
or leather were used.

230
00:18:59,606 --> 00:19:04,220
During the Middle Ages, athletes like the knights who engaged in sport

231
00:19:04,221 --> 00:19:10,420
for the amusement of kings wore krakows, pointed shoes made from whalebone and leather which were

232
00:19:10,421 --> 00:19:13,309
strapped or chained to the leg.

233
00:19:13,310 --> 00:19:18,900
In the 18th and 19th century, shoes for athletes modernized.

234
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:25,620
Foot gear became more low-cut, allowing for the greatest flexibility and maximum protection.

235
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:34,820
In 1908, a simple rubber-soled shoe was designed to give cushioning to people playing tennis, hence the tennis shoe.

236
00:19:35,180 --> 00:19:43,180
It was also at this time that the Converse Shoe Company started making its famous high-top rubber-soled shoe for basketball players.

237
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:46,460
The high-top helped prevent ankles from twisting.

238
00:19:46,461 --> 00:19:52,560
Converse was so proud of its shoes' ability to be washed in a washing machine and then

239
00:19:52,561 --> 00:19:57,980
worn, not to mention the shoe's durability, that the company proved its claims not on

240
00:19:57,981 --> 00:20:00,940
a playing field, but on the water.

241
00:20:00,941 --> 00:20:09,140
If you go back 20 or 30 years ago, the shoes were vulcanized rubber outsoles with a canvas

242
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:10,140
upper.

243
00:20:10,141 --> 00:20:16,340
There would be no cushioning, no support, so they were pretty crude.

244
00:20:16,341 --> 00:20:22,100
with the explosion of sports in the last half of the 20th century, designers look for better

245
00:20:22,101 --> 00:20:24,919
ways to protect the feet during sports.

246
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:28,460
Today there are more than a dozen different types

247
00:20:28,461 --> 00:20:29,598
of sports shoes.

248
00:20:29,599 --> 00:20:34,720
Cross trainers, walking shoes, basketball shoes, each serving a specific

249
00:20:34,721 --> 00:20:35,720
need.

250
00:20:35,721 --> 00:20:40,841
Each shoe has its own basis and its own preventative maintenance.

251
00:20:40,842 --> 00:20:43,520
Tennis shoes you have more padding

252
00:20:43,521 --> 00:20:48,460
and protection across the toe, especially those who drag their toe, and to protect the

253
00:20:48,461 --> 00:20:53,831
foot. Soccer shoes, they tend to be more conforming to the foot.

254
00:20:53,832 --> 00:20:56,600
Wrestlers don't have much padding

255
00:20:56,601 --> 00:21:01,580
to their shoes at all, but they have a good rubber sole to them so they can grab the floor

256
00:21:01,581 --> 00:21:03,479
and the mat appropriately.

257
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:08,080
Running shoes have a good heel counter and a good arch support.

258
00:21:08,081 --> 00:21:18,805
If you go to today with the cushioning, which is far superior, you go to the stability of the upper that are designed to provide that kind of stability molded to the

259
00:21:18,806 --> 00:21:21,860
foot, you're combining the best of both worlds.

260
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:26,340
Great cushioning and stability so you're going to be less injury prone.

261
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:31,260
Every foot is unique and is accustomed to different surfaces.

262
00:21:31,261 --> 00:21:37,760
The one thing we find out every time we introduce a new shoe or go out and test it with the athletes is how different each athlete is.

263
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:41,980
So when we realize that not only are athletes feet a different shape,

264
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:43,700
but every athlete runs differently,

265
00:21:44,100 --> 00:21:46,420
every athlete has had different types of injuries over their life.

266
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:48,880
So every time we develop new technology,

267
00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:51,860
we are reminded how different everybody is.

268
00:21:51,861 --> 00:21:55,880
And we have to go back and invent technologies that can adapt to an athlete.

269
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:59,640
Sometimes runners are used to wearing no shoe at all.

270
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:03,740
Some Africans would run marathons in their bare feet.

271
00:22:04,700 --> 00:22:06,480
They ran barefoot at home.

272
00:22:06,481 --> 00:22:10,920
so their feet were prepared for the pounding on the ground

273
00:22:10,921 --> 00:22:14,960
versus the rest of us here in the United States who wear shoes practically every day.

274
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:17,780
We won't be able to run on asphalt

275
00:22:17,781 --> 00:22:23,320
because we don't have the protection for the bones and the soft tissues.

276
00:22:23,820 --> 00:22:29,100
One of the biggest decisions in making a sports shoe is choosing what materials to use.

277
00:22:29,101 --> 00:22:38,180
In the mid-1970s, shoe companies began experimenting with new shock-absorbing materials in their shoes.

278
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:44,860
Simple foam has now been teamed with space-age polymers, gels, and even air.

279
00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:51,360
Here in the heel area, which is where you'll actually land at four times your body weight when you're running,

280
00:22:51,361 --> 00:22:59,600
We put in a gel, a silicone gel pad in this area to really absorb impact, the heavy forces of impact.

281
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:02,740
And here you can see on the bottom, you can actually see the gel coming through.

282
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,620
We also put one in the forefoot or the front of the shoe.

283
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:08,900
So when you push off, that's also absorbing the shock.

284
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:13,960
But finding what materials and designs work best isn't easy.

285
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:20,360
Shoe companies will conduct surveys of people who buy their shoes to find out what's working and what isn't.

286
00:23:20,361 --> 00:23:27,140
They'll then go back to their design teams to try to incorporate new engineering to improve

287
00:23:27,141 --> 00:23:30,260
on what already exists.

288
00:23:30,261 --> 00:23:35,540
We spend a lot of time out on the road with bags of shoes and stopping people on the street

289
00:23:35,541 --> 00:23:37,920
and saying, would you mind trying these on for us?

290
00:23:37,921 --> 00:23:41,600
Runners running by, here we have some new shoes for you to try.

291
00:23:41,601 --> 00:23:44,080
We go down to the basketball courts where the players are.

292
00:23:44,081 --> 00:23:48,480
So it's really going out to where the athletes are, getting them to try the shoes on.

293
00:23:48,481 --> 00:23:49,920
But we do it in a very controlled way.

294
00:23:49,921 --> 00:23:51,800
It's not just, hey, what do you think, how do you like the color?

295
00:23:52,120 --> 00:23:55,280
We do mixed pairs, so they have one shoe on one foot, another shoe on the other foot.

296
00:23:55,560 --> 00:23:58,500
Oftentimes, in fact, we can cover the shoe so they can't see what the shoes are.

297
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:01,920
We try to remove the influence of how the shoe looks from their feedback.

298
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:06,340
This is one of the smoothest scans we've done in a long time.

299
00:24:08,020 --> 00:24:12,940
And nearly every aspect of shoe technology today is generated on computers.

300
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:16,340
Everything is CAD, or computer-aided design.

301
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:19,860
We've taken that over to interface with the factory level,

302
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:26,700
so a lot of the sample molds that are done that give us the design of the midsole and the outsole

303
00:24:26,701 --> 00:24:32,980
are brought straight over from the CAD, and they'll be machined out by computer-aided machines.

304
00:24:33,600 --> 00:24:39,060
At Reebok's Development Center, shoes and athletes are put to the test.

305
00:24:39,680 --> 00:24:43,540
It's our facility where we can bring athletes in and measure their performance

306
00:24:43,541 --> 00:24:46,540
and measure the effect of shoe design on their performance.

307
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:49,240
We also, of course, have tools that measure the shoes by themselves,

308
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:52,700
but we have tools that measure impact forces of the athlete in the ground,

309
00:24:53,160 --> 00:24:54,580
pressure inside the shoe.

310
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:58,080
We have systems that measure shock in the skeletal system as an athlete's running.

311
00:24:58,940 --> 00:25:02,120
We have high-speed video cameras to record the movements,

312
00:25:02,380 --> 00:25:04,460
and we even have computer modeling equipment

313
00:25:04,461 --> 00:25:08,480
that allows us to simulate how an athlete might run or jump in a new shoe design.

314
00:25:08,481 --> 00:25:14,480
One new design being incorporated into shoes is called Podular Technology.

315
00:25:14,481 --> 00:25:19,480
In an effort to minimize shock, the shoe is broken down into several sections,

316
00:25:19,481 --> 00:25:23,480
each with its own ability to absorb the impact with the ground.

317
00:25:23,481 --> 00:25:29,480
This is a shoe called the Gecko, and you can see that the Podular configurations enhances traction,

318
00:25:29,481 --> 00:25:36,480
and it also provides additional cushioning for use on trails and uneven surfaces

319
00:25:36,481 --> 00:25:40,000
where there might be sharp rocks and sharp terrain,

320
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:43,320
this will be very well cushioned and have very good flexibility.

321
00:25:44,360 --> 00:25:48,520
One of our other applications of Podgeware technology is for cushioning.

322
00:25:49,820 --> 00:25:51,020
And this outsole is different.

323
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:56,240
And again, the number of pods and the way they're configured is different to enhance cushioning.

324
00:25:56,241 --> 00:26:00,040
So we have a stability application.

325
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:03,700
We have a performance application.

326
00:26:03,701 --> 00:26:10,700
application, we have a trail running application, and we have a cushioning application.

327
00:26:10,701 --> 00:26:14,171
The air is moving under this foot, but not under this foot.

328
00:26:14,172 --> 00:26:15,700
It's just separate pods of

329
00:26:15,701 --> 00:26:20,406
foam. And what this allowed us to do is look at what the impact forces would be.

330
00:26:20,407 --> 00:26:21,107
These

331
00:26:21,108 --> 00:26:23,843
arrows represent the impact force, and also how the foot's moving.

332
00:26:23,844 --> 00:26:24,700
We can look at how

333
00:26:24,701 --> 00:26:27,700
one joint moves relative to another.

334
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:37,300
Competition in the billion-dollar shoe industry is severe.

335
00:26:38,040 --> 00:26:42,080
Companies use extensive advertising to get the attention of consumers

336
00:26:42,081 --> 00:26:45,960
in an effort to put their shoes at the front of the pack.

337
00:26:50,780 --> 00:26:56,000
Some of today's most famous and expensive television commercials are made by shoe companies.

338
00:26:56,900 --> 00:27:02,060
In this Reebok commercial, the runner wearing the company's shoes sets himself apart from the field.

339
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:17,700
What we're seeing is the industry is coming to a new level of technology where there really

340
00:27:17,701 --> 00:27:21,700
are significant differences between the shoes beyond the way they look and beyond just the

341
00:27:21,701 --> 00:27:24,700
fit that one company may have compared to another company.

342
00:27:24,701 --> 00:27:30,200
Though the shoe industry has been at the forefront of incorporating new technology

343
00:27:30,201 --> 00:27:35,960
into their products. Many in the industry believe that they've hit a wall. There are

344
00:27:35,961 --> 00:27:38,580
fewer and fewer new ways to make a better shoe.

345
00:27:39,580 --> 00:27:43,820
Well, one of the problems our industry has right now is that we have a couple cushioning

346
00:27:43,821 --> 00:27:47,899
technologies during the industry. Certainly at Asics, we have our gel cushioning.

347
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:48,600
Nike

348
00:27:48,601 --> 00:27:49,301
has their air.

349
00:27:49,302 --> 00:27:54,840
And we have found that these are so good that to make a change, we have

350
00:27:54,841 --> 00:27:57,756
to be very clear that the change is an improvement.

351
00:27:57,757 --> 00:28:00,100
And from our standpoint, we haven't found

352
00:28:00,101 --> 00:28:05,363
something that can really give us a beneficial increase. We continue to look.

353
00:28:05,364 --> 00:28:06,320
We continue to

354
00:28:06,321 --> 00:28:12,040
look at materials and designs to push the envelope, but we want to make sure we don't take a step back,

355
00:28:12,041 --> 00:28:13,986
and that's our biggest dilemma.

356
00:28:13,987 --> 00:28:17,500
But as new advances are made in plastics and synthetics,

357
00:28:18,020 --> 00:28:22,387
other sports continue to push the limits of their equipment.

358
00:28:22,388 --> 00:28:25,080
Next, those advances are being played

359
00:28:25,081 --> 00:28:31,120
out every fall with each new season of American football.

360
00:28:31,121 --> 00:28:35,380
Today's running shoes are rated much like the tread on car tires.

361
00:28:35,381 --> 00:28:39,800
A higher rated running shoe will have more cushioning and stronger soles that make the

362
00:28:39,801 --> 00:28:44,080
shoe last more miles.

363
00:28:44,081 --> 00:28:49,340
Football, a sport of violence in which players sustain crushing impact injuries while trying

364
00:28:49,341 --> 00:28:52,600
to gain ground on an opponent.

365
00:28:52,601 --> 00:28:54,520
Technological challenge?

366
00:28:54,521 --> 00:28:57,760
find lightweight materials that better prevent injury.

367
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:04,840
In the late 1800s, the colleges of the Ivy League

368
00:29:04,841 --> 00:29:07,340
played the first American football games.

369
00:29:08,660 --> 00:29:11,120
The matches were rough and tumble affairs.

370
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:14,880
Players had little protection against the battering they took on the field.

371
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:20,940
Protective gear was so primitive that players relied on their own hair for padding.

372
00:29:20,941 --> 00:29:27,880
It may be myth, but golden locks of hair were really noted as the first form of head protection.

373
00:29:27,881 --> 00:29:34,500
Players would let their hair grow extremely long, and in a sense that served as head protection.

374
00:29:34,501 --> 00:29:38,980
Players quickly learned that no matter how thick their hair, it would not stop the pain

375
00:29:38,981 --> 00:29:42,960
and injury of a severe blow to the head.

376
00:29:42,961 --> 00:29:47,100
During the first part of the 20th century, the first helmets were developed, a skull

377
00:29:47,101 --> 00:29:49,980
cap of leather with a wool lining.

378
00:29:49,981 --> 00:29:55,480
While ineffective against crushing blows, the caps did prevent cuts on the head.

379
00:29:55,481 --> 00:29:57,220
It was a start.

380
00:29:57,221 --> 00:30:03,400
The success of these helmets led to other protective gear for early football players.

381
00:30:03,401 --> 00:30:05,540
Then it went to simple ear protection.

382
00:30:05,541 --> 00:30:09,820
You could buy one ear protection or two ear protection just to cover the ears with an

383
00:30:09,821 --> 00:30:15,420
elastic strap or a string that held those things together over your ears.

384
00:30:15,421 --> 00:30:22,040
at that time was a simple nose plate that covered the nose with shoestrings and that

385
00:30:22,041 --> 00:30:27,320
was basically the simplest form of head protection in the sport of football.

386
00:30:27,321 --> 00:30:32,340
One of the most profound changes in technology for the game of football came during the late

387
00:30:32,341 --> 00:30:37,156
1930s when engineers developed the suspension helmet.

388
00:30:37,157 --> 00:30:40,700
For the first time, the leather padding

389
00:30:40,701 --> 00:30:43,760
on a player's head was suspended away from the skull,

390
00:30:44,480 --> 00:30:45,560
diminishing an impact.

391
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:49,100
In the late 30s, as other things started to evolve,

392
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:51,000
the interior components of the helmet

393
00:30:51,001 --> 00:30:52,780
went to a suspension format,

394
00:30:53,040 --> 00:30:55,440
which was an interior webbing design

395
00:30:55,441 --> 00:31:02,000
of a central patch with webs of cloth sewn to that

396
00:31:02,001 --> 00:31:05,620
to create a gap between the top of the head

397
00:31:05,621 --> 00:31:06,720
and the actual helmet.

398
00:31:06,880 --> 00:31:09,200
So it was suspended the helmet off of the head.

399
00:31:09,201 --> 00:31:16,200
A decade later, during World War II, plastics were created and introduced to the world of sports.

400
00:31:16,201 --> 00:31:21,200
Helmets were made of hard plastics.

401
00:31:21,201 --> 00:31:29,200
They were made spherical, not because of the shape of the head, but the circular form was better at deflecting blows.

402
00:31:29,201 --> 00:31:37,200
When plastic helmets started to become popular, there were still many throwbacks who continued wanting to wear the leather helmets.

403
00:31:37,201 --> 00:31:43,960
And it wasn't until the 60s when virtually every player in the game was wearing a plastic-shelled helmet.

404
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:49,220
Plastics were also employed as a protective coating to shoulder pads and padding.

405
00:31:49,840 --> 00:31:53,200
The image of the modern football player was taking shape.

406
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:59,240
You were creating a person who was dressed a little bit like the Knights of the Middle Ages.

407
00:31:59,241 --> 00:32:03,060
not only that, with the development and training,

408
00:32:03,480 --> 00:32:07,200
the machines that became available to body build,

409
00:32:07,800 --> 00:32:10,880
with the changes in eating habits

410
00:32:10,881 --> 00:32:16,840
so that young people were not only looking better,

411
00:32:16,960 --> 00:32:19,160
they were looking bigger and being bigger.

412
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:23,400
In 1974, a third revolution took place

413
00:32:23,401 --> 00:32:25,340
in football helmet technology.

414
00:32:25,341 --> 00:32:26,041
Dr.

415
00:32:26,042 --> 00:32:34,340
Richard Schneider from the University of Michigan improved the suspension system by adding an airliner inside a helmet.

416
00:32:34,341 --> 00:32:40,340
The airliner further dispersed the shock of impact and gave the player a near perfect fit.

417
00:32:40,341 --> 00:32:49,340
This created a glove-like feel and a very nice fit for the player upon his head, also very comfortable.

418
00:32:49,341 --> 00:32:50,631
very comfortable.

419
00:32:50,632 --> 00:32:56,480
Our helmet has vent holes all throughout the top of the shell, which releases

420
00:32:56,481 --> 00:32:59,660
the heat from perspiration and from playing the game.

421
00:32:59,661 --> 00:33:02,360
If that heat were trapped in there, then that

422
00:33:02,361 --> 00:33:06,392
causes player fatigue. But by putting those vent holes in, the heat escapes.

423
00:33:06,393 --> 00:33:07,400
Thus, in the fourth

424
00:33:07,401 --> 00:33:10,981
quarter, the player is still ready to go.

425
00:33:10,982 --> 00:33:15,260
But again, the comfort section of the helmet that

426
00:33:15,261 --> 00:33:21,140
that this airliner provides really was an innovation in that the player with a

427
00:33:21,141 --> 00:33:25,220
proper fitted helmet he could worry about playing the game making the play

428
00:33:25,221 --> 00:33:30,980
catching the pass to score the touchdown versus having his helmet joggling around

429
00:33:30,981 --> 00:33:38,000
his head. Today's helmets are put through extreme durability tests to ensure that

430
00:33:38,001 --> 00:33:44,240
when a player straps one on it's going to work. We do drop tests on each helmet

431
00:33:44,241 --> 00:33:54,088
When we're looking at different plastics to see what works best, our helmets are set on rooftops in 100 degree heat because football is played in many, many, many

432
00:33:54,089 --> 00:33:57,797
different climates. We also do cold tests. We have a freezer.

433
00:33:57,798 --> 00:34:02,660
Helmets are in freezers, then they're dropped as cold to test for cold cracking.

434
00:34:02,661 --> 00:34:07,980
And there have been other changes to helmets that have improved safety and the players'

435
00:34:07,981 --> 00:34:11,540
performance, most notably the face guard.

436
00:34:11,541 --> 00:34:16,340
The first face guards, a single wire wrapped in athletic tape, were developed during the

437
00:34:16,341 --> 00:34:23,460
1930s as a way to protect players with broken noses from sustaining additional injury.

438
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:29,260
Since then, face guards have evolved into 64 different styles, each designed for a wide

439
00:34:29,261 --> 00:34:35,580
range of purposes and positions. This typically would be a linebacker's face

440
00:34:35,581 --> 00:34:39,750
guard. It's a reinforced top bar. There's a double bar at the top.

441
00:34:39,751 --> 00:34:41,140
This used to be called

442
00:34:41,141 --> 00:34:47,478
the bull ring, and that was invented to keep fingers from going into this open area.

443
00:34:47,479 --> 00:34:48,179
That

444
00:34:48,180 --> 00:34:49,136
was to stop that down.

445
00:34:49,137 --> 00:34:53,020
Larry Zonka made that popular when he played with the Dolphins.

446
00:34:53,021 --> 00:34:56,800
This is jaw protection as well as oral protection.

447
00:34:57,840 --> 00:35:01,840
There's also a specific guard made for eyeglass protection.

448
00:35:02,140 --> 00:35:05,760
It's got a vertical bar here and on this side too where it's opening,

449
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:08,580
but it was made specifically for the player that wore eyeglasses.

450
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:10,480
Eric Dickerson made that popular.

451
00:35:11,020 --> 00:35:17,740
But the technological push has given players something that experts say they should avoid at all costs,

452
00:35:18,160 --> 00:35:20,440
a feeling of being invincible.

453
00:35:20,441 --> 00:35:26,780
The downside is that the head is connected to the rest of the body with the neck, and

454
00:35:26,781 --> 00:35:32,220
the neck is the limiting factor, so when they go into a pile head down or head first, they

455
00:35:32,221 --> 00:35:37,940
can get catastrophic injuries, just because the protection of their face and their head

